data.content:
`SafeAreaEdges="None"` handles iOS safe area insets, but you likely also need to:

1. **Hide the navigation bar** if you're inside a `NavigationPage` or `Shell`:
   ```xml
   <ContentPage NavigationPage.HasNavigationBar="False"
                Shell.NavBarIsVisible="False"
                SafeAreaEdges="None">
   ```

2. **Enable edge-to-edge on Android** — starting with Android 15 (and .NET 9+ MAUI), you must explicitly draw behind system bars. In your `MainActivity.cs`:
   ```csharp
   protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
   {
       base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);
       if (OperatingSystem.IsAndroidVersionAtLeast(35))
       {
           Window!.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false);
       }
   }
   ```
   Or for all API levels, in `MauiProgram.cs`:
   ```csharp
   builder.ConfigureLifecycleEvents(events =>
   {
       events.AddAndroid(android => android.OnCreate((activity, _) =>
       {
           activity.Window!.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false);
       }));
   });
   ```

3. **Make system bars transparent** in your `Platforms/Android/Resources/values/styles.xml`:
   ```xml
   <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
   <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
   ```

4. **Ensure your Image fills the space**:
   ```xml
   <Image Source="photo.jpg" Aspect="AspectFill"
          VerticalOptions="Fill" HorizontalOptions="Fill" />
   ```

**TL;DR**: `SafeAreaEdges="None"` only controls iOS insets. On Android you must separately opt into edge-to-edge rendering via `SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(false)` and transparent system bar colors.
